{"items":[{"targetId":"n0000166","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The Birkhoff polytope B_d is the set of d×d doubly stochastic matrices: matrices with nonnegative entries whose every row and column sums to one.","densityScore":0.92,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the feasible matrix set used by later spectral claims."}]},{"targetId":"n0000167","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The Karpelevič region of order d is the set of complex numbers that can occur as eigenvalues of d×d stochastic matrices.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the spectral feasibility region referenced by the method."}]},{"targetId":"n0000170","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"B_3\\B_2 denotes the set difference: elements belonging to B_3 that do not belong to B_2.","densityScore":0.74,"relation":"fills","reason":"Makes the exclusion in the comparison mathematically explicit."}]},{"targetId":"n0000171","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"B_2 is the two-dimensional Birkhoff polytope, consisting of all 2×2 doubly stochastic matrices.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"fills","reason":"Specializes the general Birkhoff-polytope definition for the referenced comparison."}]},{"targetId":"n0000205","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The Birkhoff polytope B_d is the convex set of all d×d doubly stochastic matrices, whose entries are nonnegative and whose rows and columns each sum to one.","densityScore":0.94,"relation":"fills","reason":"Provides the prerequisite feasible-set definition."}]},{"targetId":"n0000206","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The Karpelevič region of order d is the set of all complex eigenvalues attainable by d×d stochastic matrices.","densityScore":0.87,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the region that constrains attainable spectra."}]},{"targetId":"n0000211","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"For matrices A∈R^{m×n} and B∈R^{p×q}, the Kronecker product A⊗B is the mp×nq block matrix whose (i,j) block equals A_{ij}B.","densityScore":0.94,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the factor-composition operation used by the construction."}]},{"targetId":"n0000267","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A Kronecker-factorized approach represents a large transformation as a Kronecker product of smaller matrices, reducing parameter and computation costs by exploiting product structure.","densityScore":0.86,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the structural compression technique needed to understand the comparison."}]},{"targetId":"n0000268","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Expressivity is the range of functions, transformations, or behaviors that a model family can represent under its architectural and parameter constraints.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the capability dimension used to compare parameterizations."}]},{"targetId":"n0000269","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Kronecker-factorized methods construct a high-dimensional operator from smaller factor matrices through Kronecker products.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"fills","reason":"Supplies the prerequisite meaning of the compared method family."}]},{"targetId":"n0000270","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Synthetic stream-mixing tasks are controlled benchmark problems in which a model must combine or route information drawn from multiple artificial input streams.","densityScore":0.79,"relation":"fills","reason":"Explains the purpose of the experimental task family."}]},{"targetId":"n0000271","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A GPT-style language model is a causal Transformer trained to predict the next token from preceding tokens, typically using self-attention with a causal mask.","densityScore":0.91,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the model architecture used as an experimental context."}]},{"targetId":"n0000272","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Manifold theory studies spaces that locally resemble Euclidean space, allowing nonlinear constrained sets to be analyzed with local coordinates and geometry.","densityScore":0.84,"relation":"fills","reason":"Provides the geometric vocabulary required by the dependent claim."}]},{"targetId":"n0000273","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A symmetry is a transformation that leaves a relevant object, constraint, or observable unchanged; exploiting it can reduce redundant degrees of freedom.","densityScore":0.83,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the invariance concept used in the construction or analysis."}]},{"targetId":"n0000274","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The Cayley transform is a rational map between matrix classes; in one common convention, Q=(I−A)(I+A)^{-1} maps an appropriate skew-symmetric matrix A to an orthogonal matrix Q when I+A is invertible.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the parameterization map while retaining its invertibility condition."}]},{"targetId":"n0000275","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The Birkhoff–von Neumann theorem states that every doubly stochastic matrix is a convex combination of permutation matrices.","densityScore":0.94,"relation":"fills","reason":"Explains why the Birkhoff polytope has permutation matrices as its extreme points."}]},{"targetId":"n0000276","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A Kronecker product combines matrices A and B into a block matrix A⊗B, with each scalar entry of A replaced by that scalar times B.","densityScore":0.91,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the matrix operation required by the factorized construction."}]},{"targetId":"n0000277","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A synthetic data task is an evaluation problem generated from a controlled process so that latent structure, difficulty, and expected behavior can be varied independently.","densityScore":0.82,"relation":"fills","reason":"Explains why the benchmark can isolate a targeted capability."}]},{"targetId":"n0000278","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"A GPT-style language model is an autoregressive Transformer that predicts each token from the preceding context using causally masked self-attention.","densityScore":0.91,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the model class referenced by the experiment."}]},{"targetId":"n0000279","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The Birkhoff–von Neumann theorem says that a doubly stochastic matrix can be expressed as a convex combination of permutation matrices.","densityScore":0.93,"relation":"fills","reason":"Provides the theorem needed to interpret the matrix decomposition claim."}]},{"targetId":"n0000280","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"Big-O notation gives an asymptotic upper bound: f(n)=O(g(n)) means that for sufficiently large n, |f(n)| is bounded above by a constant multiple of |g(n)|.","densityScore":0.9,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the complexity notation used in the scaling claim."}]},{"targetId":"n0000281","injections":[{"type":"definition","text":"The factorial n! is the product of all positive integers from 1 through n, with 0!=1.","densityScore":0.88,"relation":"fills","reason":"Defines the combinatorial quantity used in the count or complexity expression."}]}]}
